          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The idea of
            %em topological quantum computation
            is to implement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+systems"} quantum systems
            whose dynamics is described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            (TQFT), so that the defining invariance of TQFTs under local perturbations implements protection of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+coherence"} quantum coherence
            by fundamental physical principles, instead of after the fact by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#GeneralIdea General idea
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong The Problem of Contemporary Quantum Computing.
          %p Common quantum computing architectures
          %blockquote
            %p
              (such as based on
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#SuperconductingQBitsReferences"} superconducting qbits
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trapped-ion+quantum+computing"} trapped ions
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "nuclear+magnetic+resonance#SpinResonanceQBitsReferences"} spin resonance
          %p suffer from an intrinsic tension:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
                are
                %strong
                  implemented via
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                between
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsystems"} subsystems
            %li
              %p
                but
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+coherence"} coherence
                requires
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong avoiding interaction
          %blockquote
            %p
              (cf.
              %a{:href => "quantum+computing#Waintal24"} Waintal 2024, p3
              or
              %a{:href => "quantum+computation#CCEHRSZ07"} CCEHRSZ 07
              p 272: “
              %em> Quantum logic gates involving two atomic qubits must overcome the problem of the short range coherent interaction of neutral atoms, while maintaining atom confinement and suppressing decoherence. The main challenge is to perform the gate sufficiently fast compared to typical decoherence and relaxation times.
              ”)
          %p
            This problem haunts contemporary
            %a{:href => "quantum+computation#ReferencesNISQ"} NISQ
            devices (cf.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "quantum+computation#HHT23"} HHT 23
          %p
            whence the “Quantum Winter” (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "quantum+computation#McKenzie2024"} McKenzie 24
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong The bold idea of Topological Quantum Computing
            is to cut this Gordian knot:
          %p
            Find
            = succeed "!" do
              %strong quantum gates operating without interaction
          %p Can this work? In principle: Yes!
          %blockquote
            %p
              (By a phenomenon known as the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"}> quantum adiabatic theorem
              ”.)
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            For this we need a quantum system (say a crystalline
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} quantum material
          %p with the following properties:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong> ground states degenracy
                \:
              %p
                even when completely frozen at
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+zero"} absolute zero
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
              %p the system has more than one state to be in (even up to phase)
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Meaning: The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
                  with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalue"} eigenvalue
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = 0
                  is of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 2
            %li
              %p
                an
                %strong energy gap
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \gt 0
              %p
                every excitation of the ground state has energy larger than
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
              %p
                so that (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light"}> light
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quanta"} quanta
                impacting the material have
                %strong no interaction
                as long as they carry energy
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi ϵ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt \epsilon
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Meaning: The material’s
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
                  has a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+gap"} spectral gap
                  above the
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            %li#ControlParameters
              %p
                %strong> control parameters
                \:
              %p
                the above properties hold for a range of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuously
                tunable parameters
              %p
                such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strain"} strain
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/voltage"} voltage
                applied to the crystal
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Meaning: We have
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi.mathscript ℋ
                          %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}_p
                  over a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                  of parameter values
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi P
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in P
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Adiabatic Transformation of Ground States.
          %p Under these conditions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p after cooling such a system to its ground state,
            %li
              %p
                sufficiently slow (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"}> adiabatic
                ”) variation of the external parameters
            %li
              %p
                does not excite the system: it
                %em remains
                in a ground state
            %li
              %p
                but
                %strong the different ground states may transform into each other
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Meaning: Each parameter path
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi p
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \,\colon\, p \to p'
                  induces a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phase"} Berry phase
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi γ
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                            %mi p
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                            %mrow
                              %mi p
                              %mo ′
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_\gamma \,\colon\, \mathscr{H}_p \xrightarrow{\;} \mathscr{H}_{p'}
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            This is part of a general phenomenon of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fluctuations"} quantum fluctuations
            are a little like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermal
            fluctuations
          %p
            one key difference is that they remain present at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+zero"} absolute zero
          %blockquote
            %p
              (Compare
              = succeed ".)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+phase+transitions"} quantum phase transitions
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Such operations on ground states are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a{:href => "adiabatic+quantum+computation#ReferencesGeometricPhaseGates"} holonomic quantum gates
          %blockquote
            %p
              (From “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"}> holonomy
              ” for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
              along
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loops"} loops
          %p
            These are
            %strong
              protected from external
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
            quanta of energy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt \epsilon
          %p
            %strong but
            may still be sensitive no
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
                in the parameter paths
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Topological invariance.
          %p
            To overcome this last issue, look for such quantum systems which
            %em in addition
            have:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong parameter topology
              %p The parameter space has “holes”, in that
              %p some closed parameter paths cannot continuously be deformed to constant paths.
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Meaning: The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
                  of parameter space is non-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"}> trivial
                  \.)
            %li
              %p
                %strong local parameter independence
              %p All parameter paths with the same endpoints
              %p that are continuous deformations of each other
              %p yield the same transformation on ground states.
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Meaning: The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
                  is
                  = succeed ".)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} Quantum materials
            with all these properties are
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topologically ordered
          %p
            the resulting adiabatic quantum processes are
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum gates
          %p
            %br/
          %p In principle, such topological quantum gates are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                protected against external
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
                quanta of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi ϵ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt \epsilon
                %em and
            %li
              %p
                protected against any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
                in the parameter path.
          %p In fact, the quantum operation induced by a parameter variation will
          %p
            %em depend only on the discrete data
            of
          %p
            %strong how much the path winds around the holes
            of parameter space
          %center
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.02382#page=21"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TopologicalGateSchematics.jpg", :width => "740"}/
          %p
            %br/
          %p This way,
          %p topological quantum architecture may in principle
          %p (be necessary to) solve the problem of quantum computing.
          %blockquote
            %p
              (cf.
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Sau17"} Sau 2017
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "topological+quantum+computation#DasSarma22"} Das Sarma 2022
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            So far this is the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong theory behind topological quantum gates
          %p On the one hand it is extremely general:
          %ul
            %li any kind of topologically parameterized quantum system could do.
          %p On the other hand it is very ambitious:
          %ul
            %li suitable such system have yet to be devised in the labs.
          %p
            %br/
          %p But the range of possibilities has hardly been explored,
          %p most attention has been given to a single approach:
          %ul
            %li braiding of anyonic defects in position space.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            That’s what we discuss
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#IdeaViaAnyonBraiding"} next
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#IdeaViaAnyonBraiding Via anyon braiding
          %p
            In specialization of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GeneralIdea"} above general idea
          %p
            original proposal due to
            %a{:href => "#Kitaev03"} Kitaev 2003
            and
            %a{:href => "#FreedmanKitaevLarsenWang03"} FKLW 2003
            (which has become canonized in the literature) is to envision
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an effectively 2-dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+material"} quantum material
                (such as an atomic monolayer similar to
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            %li
              %p
                with some
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect"} defect
                  points
                of sorts (whose expected nature in real materials remains vague at this point),
            %li
              %p
                whose positions constitute the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#ControlParameters"} above parameters
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
                in the plane.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motion+group"} motion
            in such a parameter space is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid"} braid
            (of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"}> worldlines
            ” of defect points) and
          %p
            if this acts non-trivially on the material’s ground states by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            transformations,
          %p
            then one refers to these defects as
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect"} defect
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"} anyons
          %center
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.00628#page=7"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AnyonBraidingSchematics.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (graphics from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Topological+QBits+in+Flux-Quantized+Supergravity"} SS24
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Status of anyonic quantum computation.
          %p Experimentally,
          %p
            at least
            %em
              %a{:href => "#FocusOnAbelianAnyons"} abelian anyons
          %blockquote
            %p
              (whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representation"} braid representation
              factors through a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+of+the+symmetric+group"} representation of the symmetric group
          %p
            %a{:href => "braid+group+statistics#ExperimentalRealization"} are seen
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+quantum+Hall+effect"} fractional quantum Hall systems
          %p though their controlled movement along the above lines seems out of reach.
          %p
            %br/
          %p#ChernSimonsTheory Theoretically,
          %p
            such defect anyons are expected to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effectively described
            by
          %p
            some kind of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              (and hence ultimately classified by
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+categories"} modular tensor categories
          %p though attempts to derive some of these expectations from microscopic physics are rare.
          %blockquote
            %p
              (A more microscopic argument for anyonic defects not in position-space but in “reciprocal” momentum-space – the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
              –, appearing there as the familiar
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/band+nodes"} band nodes
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+semimetals"} topological semimetals
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Anyonic+topological+order+in+TED+K-theory"} SS23
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong The open problem of strongly-coupled quantum systems.
          %p A general problem on both of these fronts is that
          %p
            anyonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            is supposed to arise in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+correlated+systems"} strongly correlated systems
            which, like
          %p
            all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative physics
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The directly analogous problem in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
          %p
            where the the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            is known as the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
          %p
            has been termed a
            %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millennium_Prize_Problems"} Millennium Problem
            by the
            %a{:href => "https://www.claymath.org/"} Clay Math Institute
          %blockquote
            %p (cf. the Mass Gap Problemap#ReferencesMassGapProblem))
          %p
            %br/
          %p One plausible approach on this front is to find
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+engineering+of+quantum+field+theory"} geometric engineering
            of anyonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-branes"} M-branes
          %p
            or their
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+in+condensed+matter+physics"} holographic dual
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} bulk gravity
          %blockquote
            %p
              (cf.
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "topological+order#CGK20"} CGK20
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "topological+order#SS24"} SS24
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Loosely related approaches.
          %p
            Much attention in the 2010s had been given to claims of experimental detection of anyons in the form of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Majorana+zero+modes"}> Majorana zero modes
            ” (MZMs) – but these claims
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Majorana+zero+mode#But"} seem not to hold water
          %p
            More recently the idea of
            %em
              %a{:href => "quantum+simulation+OfAnyons"}
                quantum
                %em simulation
                of anyons
            on ordinary quantum hardware has found more attention, though the relevance of this, if any, to the original idea of quantum-error protection by fundamental physical principles may remain to be discussed.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Topological quantum protocols.
          %p
            Topological quantum computation protocols with defect anyons are often assumed to start by creating anyon/anti-anyon pairs out of the “anyon
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link"} link
            which, when regarded as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop"} Wilson loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observable"} quantum observable
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            mentioned
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#ChernSimonsTheory"} above
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px -10px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TQCOnAnyonsIdea.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                Adapted from
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#RowellWang18"} Rowell  Wang 18
                %a{:href => "#Rouabah20"} Rouabah 20
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            In any case, in this scheme the topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            are encoded by
            = succeed "-elements" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
            on the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px -10px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumGatesAsBraidGroupRepresentations.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"} (from Sati-Schreiber 21)
          %div{:style => "margin: 0px 0px -10px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumBraidGateI.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#LahtinenPachos17"} Lahtinen-Pachos 17
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Topological Quantum Compilation.
          %p
            This means that quantum gates based on anyon braiding are (going to be) quite different from the
            %a{:href => "quantum+logic+gate#Examples"} standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            traditionally considered in
            = succeed "-based" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbit"} qbit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
          %p#BraidImplementingCNotGate
            For example, the following lengthy braid has been proposed &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#HormoziZikosBonesteelSimon07"}> Hormozi, Zikos, Bonesteel &amp; Simon (2007)
            \&amp;rbrack; as a possible topological implementation of a
            %em single
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CNOT+gate"} CNOT gate
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/BraidImplementingCNOTGate.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %p
            Hence if and when such topological quantum hardware becomes available, a major issue on the quantum software side will be the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+compilation"} compilation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+algorithms"} quantum algorithms
            to the exotic-seeming machine-level gates (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "/nlab/show/Solovay-Kitaev+theorem#ReferencesInTopologicalQuantumComputation"} topological quantum compilation
          %p
            It
            %a{:href => "certified+programming#ReferencesForQuantumProgrammingLanguages"} has been argued
            that the complexity of this process will make its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/certified+programming"} formal verification
            a practical necessity.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#extended_tqc Extended TQC?
          %p It may be interesting to note that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+braid+group"}> pure
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representations"} braid group representations
                are equivalently degree-1
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
                (ordered) in the
                = succeed ",;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+plane"} Euclidean plane
            %li
              %p
                as such,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
                are the first stage in a sequence that continues with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems"} weight systems
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+chord+diagrams"} horizontal chord diagrams
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cohomology"} complex cohomology
                in higher degree of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
                (ordered) in Euclideam 3-space (see at
                = succeed "):" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems+are+cohomology+of+loop+space+of+configuration+space"} weight systems are cohomology of loop space of configuration space
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px -10px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BraidRepresentationsAndWeightSystemsAnalogy.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"} from Sati-Schreiber 2021
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Conf
                        %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi PBr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi B
                      %mi PBr
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
