          %h2#References References
          %h3#ReferencesNeedForTopologicalProtection Need for topological protection
          %p Highlighting the need for topological stabilization mechanisms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FreedmanKitaevLarsenWang03"}> Freedman, Kitaev, Larsen &amp; Wang 2003
                \:
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “the chief advantage of anyonic [topological quantum] computation would be physical error correction: An error rate scaling like
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi e
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mi α
                              %mi ℓ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{- \alpha \ell}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℓ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell
                  is a length scale, and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
                  is some positive constant. In contrast, the “presumptive” qubit-model of quantum computation, which repairs errors combinatorically, requires a fantastically low initial error rate (about
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mn 10
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 4
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{-4}
            %li#Sau17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jay+Sau"}> Jay Sau
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Roadmap for a Scalable Topological Quantum Computer
                %a{:href => "https://physics.aps.org/articles/v10/68"}
                  Physics
                  %strong 10
                  68 (2017)
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “small machines are unlikely to uncover truly macroscopic quantum phenomena, which have no classical analogs. This will likely require a scalable approach to quantum computation. &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; based on &amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; topological quantum computation (TQC) as envisioned by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Kitaev"} Alexei Kitaev
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Freedman"} Michael Freedman
                  \&amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; The central idea of TQC is to encode
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubits"} qubits
                  into states of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            %li#DasSarma22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sankar+Das+Sarma"}> Sankar Das Sarma
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum computing has a hype problem
                %a{:href => "https://www.technologyreview.com/2022/03/28/1048355/quantum-computing-has-a-hype-problem/"} MIT Technology Review (March 2022)
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “The qubit systems we have today are a tremendous scientific achievement, but they take us no closer to having a quantum computer that can solve a problem that anybody cares about. […] What is missing is the breakthrough […] bypassing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
                  by using far-more-stable qubits, in an approach called topological quantum computing.”
          %div
